        "original_id": "da7066ea-aa96-5bf8-8229-3702c6e47b09",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Cases: Phillips v. Martin Marietta Corp.; Roth v. United States; Miller v. California\n- Courts: United States Supreme Court\n- Laws: Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964; First Amendment\n- Concepts: business necessity; obscene material\n\nFacts:\n- In 1957, Roth v. United States, 354 U.S. 476, was a landmark United States Supreme Court case that redefined the constitutional test for determining what obscene material is unprotected by the First Amendment.\n- Roth v. United States was described along with its companion case Miller v. California.\n- In 1971, Phillips v. Martin Marietta Corp., 400 U.S. 542, was a United States Supreme Court case about Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964.\n- Phillips v. Martin Marietta Corp. held that, absent business necessity, an employer may not refuse to hire women with pre-school-age children while hiring men with such children.",
